</head>
<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Engineering Notebook home"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 4h6v6H4zM14 4h6v6h-6zM4 14h6v6H4zM14 14h6v6h-6z"/></svg><span class="nav-label">Engineering Notebook</span></a>
    <nav aria-label="Primary navigation"><a class="active" href="/projects/"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 5.5 4h5l2 2h6A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 20 7.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-13A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4 18.5z"/></svg><span class="nav-label">Projects</span></a><a href="/home-server/"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 4h14a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14H4V5a1 1 0 0 1 1-1Zm0 4h14M8 12h2m4 0h2m-8 3h2m4 0h2"/></svg><span class="nav-label">Home server</span></a><a href="/notes/"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 4h12a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14H5V5a1 1 0 0 1 1-1Zm2 4h8M8 12h8M8 16h5"/></svg><span class="nav-label">Notes</span></a></nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section class="intro section-shell"><p class="eyebrow">PROJECTS / BUILD LOGS</p><h1>Small systems, real constraints.</h1><p class="lede">These projects are built in public-sized steps, with the tests, tradeoffs, failures, and next questions left visible.</p><button class="intro-scroll-cue" type="button" aria-label="Scroll to next section"></button></section>
    <section class="section-shell"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / CURRENT MILESTONE COMPLETE</p><h2>Flashpoint</h2><p class="section-intro">An inventory and order service built from a small HTTP endpoint into a measured, observable workload.</p></div><article class="feature-card"><div class="card-kicker">JAVA 21 · DOCKER · PROMETHEUS · M1 COMPLETE · INTERACTIVE SYSTEM MAP</div><h3>The engineering problem</h3><p>Concurrent orders can oversell inventory. Retries can create duplicate orders. A traffic spike can exhaust resources. A service can look healthy while its latency or error rate quietly changes.</p><p>The first milestone is complete and privately deployed. It has repeatable tests, a demo command, load measurements, Docker packaging, Prometheus metrics, and Grafana dashboards.</p><p>The public interaction is planned as a separate, controlled surface. It will be rate-limited and isolated from the private deployment, administration surface, and monitoring systems.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>Idempotency</span><span>Concurrency</span><span>Rate limiting</span><span>Load testing</span></div><div class="card-footer"><FlashpointFlow client:visible /><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pjk-builds/flashpoint" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View repository ↗</a></div></article></section>
    <section class="section-shell split-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / PLATFORM PROJECT</p><h2>Home Server</h2></div><div class="homelab-grid"><div class="homelab-copy"><p>A private platform for learning hardware, Linux, remote access, Docker, UPS protection, monitoring, backups, and recovery.</p><p>The server gives the software projects a real place to run while keeping the operating boundary private.</p><a class="text-link" href="/home-server/">Open the home-server story →</a></div><div class="system-map" aria-label="Project platform flow"><div><strong>Build</strong><small>hardware and Ubuntu</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div><strong>Protect</strong><small>SSH, UFW, Tailscale, UPS</small></div><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span><div><strong>Observe</strong><small>Prometheus, Grafana, alerts</small></div></div></div></section>
    <section class="section-shell"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / ROADMAP</p><h2>Each milestone adds one systems problem and a test for it.</h2></div><div class="note-grid"><article class="note-card"><span>01 · PUBLIC INTERACTION</span><h3>Open the demo carefully</h3><p>Visitors will be able to interact with a controlled Flashpoint demo while the private deployment, administration surface, and monitoring remain isolated.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>02 · M2 · DURABLE STATE</span><h3>Make state survive restarts</h3><p>Move inventory and orders from process memory to transactional PostgreSQL, with conditional stock updates, unique idempotency keys, and restart tests.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>03 · M3 · REQUEST-STORM PROTECTION</span><h3>Control traffic across instances</h3><p>Explore Redis-backed distributed rate limiting, bounded admission queues, retry budgets, jitter, and load profiles for spikes and retry storms.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>04 · M4 · ASYNCHRONOUS FULFILLMENT</span><h3>Separate order work safely</h3><p>Introduce order events and workers with at-least-once delivery, idempotent consumers, dead-letter handling, and replay tooling.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>05 · M5 · OPERABILITY AND SCALE</span><h3>See and operate more instances</h3><p>Add traces, service-level objectives, multi-instance correctness tests, readiness checks, and graceful shutdown before exploring orchestration.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>06 · LOCAL EXPERIMENTS</span><h3>AI with guardrails</h3><p>A future local AI experiment will respect resource limits, privacy, observability, and the private network boundary.</p></article></div></section>
  </main>
  <footer class="site-footer section-shell"><span>Engineering Notebook</span><span>Personal experiments, documented carefully.</span></footer>
<script is:inline src="/scroll-cue.js"></script></body>
</html>
